    22	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    23	# =====================================
    24	# a) Concrete behavior and trace
    25	#    - Scene: one winding blue river on a green background, plus two solid squares
    26	#      of different sizes. The smaller square is the mover, the larger square is
    27	#      the destination region. Colors are not role-defining.
    28	#    - Concrete trace:
    29	#      original task: two same-sized 3x3 squares -> solver groups same size,
    30	#      sorts by color id, moves the color-smaller square toward the other 3x3.
    31	#      combo task: a 6x6 square and a 7x7 square on a 28x28 grid -> solver must
    32	#      ignore color order, pick the 6x6 as mover, run BFS with a 6x6 footprint,
    33	#      and accept any reachable 6x6 top-left fully contained inside the 7x7
    34	#      target. If a choke narrows below 6, the mover stops at the farthest
    35	#      reachable 6x6 placement instead of entering the target.
    36	#    - Difference summary: D8 breaks the old color-order identity rule, while D9
    37	#      makes the mover footprint large enough that chokepoints become visually
    38	#      strong and materially affect reachability.
    39	# b) Complexity calibration
    40	#    - To keep the area cue obvious but controlled, sizes come from only three
    41	#      pairs: (5,6), (6,7), (7,8). The mover size N is always 5..7, matching the
    42	#      large-size requirement; the destination is exactly one size larger.
    43	#    - Grid size stays in 25..30 and correlates with mover size, so large squares
    44	#      occupy a meaningful fraction of the board without overfilling it.
    45	#    - Object colors are sampled independently from a fixed palette, so examples
    46	#      repeatedly show that size, not color id, determines roles.
    47	# c) Implicit assumption audit of main()
    48	#    - Solid-square detection still assumes monochrome filled squares. Generator
    49	#      guarantees that both object components are perfect squares.
    50	#    - Original code assumed at least two squares of the same size. That breaks
    51	#      here, so role selection is rewritten to require one unique smallest square
    52	#      and one unique largest square.
    53	#    - Original target logic assumed a single landing top-left equal to the
    54	#      destination square's top-left. That breaks because the target is larger;
    55	#      solver now enumerates all mover-sized windows fully inside the target bbox.
    56	#    - BFS feasibility still assumes movement is determined solely by the mover
    57	#      footprint. This remains valid because the generator carves the river using
    58	#      the mover size N, not the destination size.
    59	#    - River-color inference still assumes there is exactly one non-background,
    60	#      non-object color left. Generator keeps only bg, river, mover, destination.
    61	# d) Placement legality
    62	#    - Generator renders the river from mover-sized placement cells, so every
    63	#      traversable position corresponds to an actual N x N legal window.
    64	#    - Destination placement is constrained to touch a border and to overlap only
    65	#      a simple terminal river strip; this avoids accidental overlap with other
    66	#      river branches and keeps all objects inside bounds.
    67	#    - Chokepoints are added before object colors are painted, and connectivity as
    68	#      well as reachability are revalidated afterward.
    69	# e) Visual observability
    70	#    - Across samples, the stable cue is that the smaller square is the one that
    71	#      moves, even when it has a numerically larger color id than the target.
    72	#    - The larger target remains partially visible after a successful move, so the
    73	#      "small square entering a larger square" relation is directly observable.
    74	#    - Big mover sizes 5..7 make narrow gates visibly narrower than the mover.
    75	# f) Visual cleanliness
    76	#    - The target overlaps only the straight terminal river strip, not arbitrary
    77	#      turns, so the final boards stay legible instead of having heavy region
    78	#      overpainting.
    79	#    - There are still only four colors per grid and exactly two non-river square
    80	#      objects, keeping the scene sparse enough to parse by eye.
    81	# g) Change inferability
    82	#    - No invisible ordering rule is used: not color id, not component order.
    83	#      Human-solvable evidence comes from repeated size differences and from the
    84	#      large mover footprint interacting with visibly narrow chokepoints.
    85	# 9) New solver assumptions and generator guarantees
    86	#    - Solver assumes exactly one smallest and one largest solid square component.
    87	#      Generator emits exactly two square objects with different sizes.
    88	#    - Solver assumes the destination square is strictly larger than the mover and
    89	#      solid, so every contained N x N window is a valid candidate landing area.
    90	#      Generator enforces size pairs with m = n + 1 and solid fill.
    91	#    - Solver assumes the mover's start top-left is itself feasible under the N x
